text:
alex: quick check — what should our SDK default retry policy be for 429s and 5xxs? also how do we handle streaming resume vs. idempotency keys for non-stream endpoints?

morgan: tl;dr: honor Retry-After for 429, otherwise exponential backoff + full jitter. cap backoff (30s) and max attempts (4-6) by default. don't retry non-idempotent POSTs unless caller provides an idempotency key. :white_check_mark: 

alex: when you say full jitter, numbers?

morgan: example we use internally: base=500ms, max=30s, attempts=5. delay = random_between(0, min(max, base * 2**attempt)). so attempt1 ~ U(0,1s), attempt2 ~ U(0,2s), etc.

rachel: streaming nuance: you can't transparently retry in the middle of a live server-sent stream. instead provide a resumable session id and last-processed token/event id so client can reconnect and resume. server should expose resumable cursor in the stream metadata.

rachel: pattern: for streaming writes/long-poll read streams, send a "session-id" header and server returns "last-event-id" periodically. on reconnect, client sends session-id + last-event-id to catch up.

sam: 5xx classification: 502/503/504 -> definitely transient, safe to retry with backoff. 500 is noisy — usually okay to retry a few times but add telemetry/alert if persistent. 429 is quota/rate limit — prefer to respect Retry-After and/or slow down batching.

alex: auth/401 case — should SDK refresh token and retry automatically?
morgan: yes, for transient 401 caused by expired tokens, SDK should attempt a single refresh+retry. but only one transparent refresh retry; if still 401, surface error to caller. don't combine refresh loops with exponential retries (avoid thundering refresh storms).

alex: any language-specific gotchas (node/python)?
morgan: Node: avoid blocking sleeps, use setTimeout/async sleep and don't block event loop. example:
```js
// naive retry helper (node fetch)
async function retryFetch(url, opts) {
  let attempt = 0;
  const base = 500;
  const max = 30000;
  while (attempt < 5) {
    const res = await fetch(url, opts);
    if (res.ok) return res;
    const retryAfter = res.headers.get('retry-after');
    if (retryAfter) {
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, parseInt(retryAfter)*1000));
      attempt++;
      continue;
    }
    if (res.status >= 500 || res.status === 429) {
      const jitter = Math.random() * Math.min(max, base * Math.pow(2, attempt));
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, jitter));
      attempt++;
      continue;
    }
    throw new Error('non-retriable ' + res.status);
  }
  throw new Error('max attempts reached');
}
```

morgan: Python: requests has backoff libs (tenacity / urllib3 Retry). make sure to set respect_retry_after_header=True and only retry on allowed methods or when idempotency-key provided for POST. also catch streaming read exceptions separately.

rachel: small note on idempotency-keys: use a strong UUID v4 or deterministic id (hash of payload+user-id) depending on semantics. server should be conservative: idempotency-key dedupe window = e.g., 24h by default, configurable.

sam: streaming resume example (very short):
```http
# request
GET /v1/streams/updates
Headers: Session-Id: abc123
# response (on reconnect)
GET /v1/streams/updates?session_id=abc123&since_event=evt_789
```

alex: what about client libraries that expose both sync and async APIs?
morgan: sync wrappers should delegate to the async core; still use non-blocking timers. for blocking sync wrappers (py), document that retries may block thread and offer an async variant.

